schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: Layer 2 Solutions
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: concept
  summary: >-
    Explains how Layer 2 networks (Base, Arbitrum, Optimism, zkSync Era,
    Starknet, Scroll) scale Ethereum by processing transactions off the main
    chain and settling compressed batches back to it, cutting fees from
    several dollars to fractions of a cent. Covers Optimistic vs. ZK rollup
    designs, why this matters for regenerative-finance projects running
    micro-transactions or community token systems, and how to get started
    (MetaMask, bridging, using L2 dApps).
  audience: regen project builders new to Ethereum scaling, East Africa / Somaliland-focused track
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      Optimistic Rollups (accessible, widely compatible, ~7-day challenge
      window) vs. ZK Rollups (faster finality, no challenge window, more
      technically complex, "still maturing")
  related_concepts:
    - Layer 2 (Ethereum scaling)
  related_resources:
    - Base
    - Arbitrum
    - L2BEAT
  maturity: raw
  public_use: source-linked-unreviewed
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/layer-2s.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted

# assessed-but-excluded:
#   - The step-by-step "Getting Started" and "Try This" instructions
#     (installing MetaMask, bridging funds, sending a test transaction) were
#     folded into the summary rather than split into a separate
#     implementation-record — they are generic onboarding steps, not a
#     documented deployment case.
#   - Optimism, zkSync Era, Starknet, Scroll, Uniswap, and Across Protocol
#     were mentioned but not each given a standalone resource object —
#     only the two most load-bearing examples (Base, Arbitrum) plus the
#     tracking tool (L2BEAT) were lifted, to avoid one-object-per-brand-
#     mention sprawl for a single 900-word article.
